#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tests/merge-check — run the suite on the MERGED result of several open
changes, and name the pair that disagrees.

    python3 tests/merge-check --base feat/work-modes --all-open -j 4
    python3 tests/merge-check --base feat/work-modes --pr 14 --pr 15
    python3 tests/merge-check --base <ref> a=<ref> b=<ref> --checks test_state_cost.py

Why this exists
---------------
On 2026-08-20 PR #14 put both store files into `claims[]` and PR #15 shipped
`tests/test_state_cost.py` asserting the world before that claim existed. Each
was fine on its own. Merged, two tests were red that **neither change could
have seen**, and a human found out by running the suite afterwards.

The obvious diagnosis is wrong, and it is worth writing down so nobody spends
the afternoon on it again. `.github/workflows/ci.yml` fires on `pull_request`,
and `actions/checkout` on a `pull_request` event checks out `refs/pull/N/merge`
— **the merge result, already**. The run for PR #16 that day says so verbatim:

    git fetch ... +9e18354...:refs/remotes/pull/16/merge
    HEAD is now at 9e18354 Merge 96822a4e... into e9d8c4dc...

So "check out the merge result instead of the head" fixes nothing; it is what
already happens. The two real defects are:

1. That merge result is computed against the base **as it stood when the event
   fired**, and GitHub does not re-fire `pull_request` when the base branch
   moves. #14 and #15 were both computed against `e9d8c4dc`. #14 landed on
   `02e61fd` and #15 landed on top of #14. No run ever saw the tree that
   shipped.
2. Nothing ever tests two *open* changes together. Each PR is merged with the
   base and with nothing else, so a pair that only disagrees with each other is
   invisible until one of them lands.

This script is the check for both: it merges the base tip with every candidate
at once and runs the suite on that tree — which is what the repository is about
to become, not what any single PR was tested as.

What it reports
---------------
"The suite is red" is not actionable, so the run does not stop there. When the
combined tree is red it re-runs the failing check three more ways:

  * on the **base alone** — a check already red before anybody's change is
    reported as pre-existing and attributed to nobody. This repository has one
    such module today, so without this step every pair would look guilty.
  * on **each candidate alone** — red there means that change is simply
    broken, and it is named on its own rather than as half a pair.
  * on **each surviving pair** — green apart, red together, is the pair. That
    differential is what makes the signal attributable instead of a shrug.

A check that is red combined but green in every pair is reported as needing
three or more changes together, with the whole set named. The mechanism says
what it cannot narrow down rather than pointing at an innocent pair.

Cost
----
The happy path is **one** suite run: if the combined tree is green, nothing
interacts and no attribution work happens. Only a red combined tree pays for
the per-check solo and pair runs, and those run one module at a time.

Note what is deliberately NOT done: candidates are never filtered by whether
their diffs touch the same files. #14 touched `schema/state-schema.json` and
`bin/perry-lint`; #15 touched `bin/perry-state-cost` and its test. **Zero
overlap.** Any "do these changes touch the same files" shortcut misses exactly
the failure this script was written for.

What this does not cover
------------------------
The sweep is `tests/parallel` plus the two `bin/perry-lint` gates — steps 2, 1
and 4 of `tests/run`. Step 3 (every shipped script compiles and answers
`--help`) is **not** mirrored here, because mirroring it means copying the list
of scripts out of `tests/run`, and a copied list that silently stops matching
its original is the defect this repository keeps finding in itself. The two
lint gates are already a mirror of that kind and are kept only because
attribution needs a per-check verdict, which `tests/run` does not expose. If
step 3 ever starts catching things a pair broke, the fix is to make `tests/run`
report per-step so this can call it — not to grow the copy.

Limits, stated rather than discovered later
-------------------------------------------
* **This reports; it does not block.** The workflow failing is a signal a
  human reads. Refusing the merge means marking `merge-check` a REQUIRED
  status check in branch protection, which is a repository-admin setting and
  deliberately not something this script or its workflow can grant itself.
  Until somebody applies it, a red run can still be merged past.
* **A flaky test can be misattributed.** Naming a pair needs the check green
  alone and red together, which a flake can satisfy by luck. The differential
  makes that much less likely than a bare re-run would, not impossible. A pair
  you cannot explain from the diffs is worth re-running before believing.
* **Pair search is quadratic** in the number of open PRs, and only pays when
  the combined tree is red. Three open PRs is three pairs; ten is forty-five.
  It is bounded per pair by a single module, not the whole suite.
* **Three-way interactions are named, not narrowed.** See above.

Isolation
---------
Merges happen in a throwaway `git clone --shared` under a temp dir. Nothing is
checked out, staged, merged or cleaned in the repository you run it from, so it
is safe to run from a worktree somebody else is working in.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import itertools
import json
import pathlib
import re
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile

ANSI = re.compile(r"\x1b\[[0-9;]*m")
RED_MODULE = re.compile(r"^✗\s+(test_\S+\.py)")

GREEN, RED, ABSENT = "green", "red", "absent"

# Checks that `tests/parallel` does not cover but `tests/run` does. They cost
# seconds, and a claim collision is exactly the kind of thing the schema drift
# guard would catch, so leaving them out would leave a hole under this script.
LINT_CHECKS = {
    "lint:templates": (
        ["python3", "bin/perry-lint", "--templates"], "bin/perry-lint"),
    "lint:fixture-en": (
        ["python3", "bin/perry-lint", "--root", "tests/fixtures/sample-project"],
        "tests/fixtures/sample-project"),
    "lint:fixture-zh": (
        ["python3", "bin/perry-lint", "--root", "tests/fixtures/sample-project-zh"],
        "tests/fixtures/sample-project-zh"),
}


def sh(args, cwd=None, check=True):
    p = subprocess.run(args, cwd=cwd, capture_output=True, text=True)
    if check and p.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(
            f"{' '.join(args)} failed ({p.returncode})\n{p.stdout}\n{p.stderr}")
    return p


def plain(text: str) -> str:
    return ANSI.sub("", text)


def note(msg: str):
    """Progress, on stderr. A CI job that prints nothing for ten minutes reads
    as a hang, and this one legitimately takes that long."""
    print(f"  … {msg}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# candidates


class Candidate:
    def __init__(self, name, ref, sha=None, pr=None, title=""):
        self.name, self.ref, self.sha, self.pr, self.title = (
            name, ref, sha, pr, title)

    def __repr__(self):
        return f"{self.name} {(self.sha or self.ref)[:8]}"


def open_prs(repo: pathlib.Path, base: str):
    """Every open PR targeting `base`, via gh. Requires GH_TOKEN in CI."""
    try:
        p = sh(["gh", "pr", "list", "--base", base, "--state", "open",
                "--limit", "50", "--json", "number,headRefOid,title,isDraft"],
               cwd=repo)
    except (RuntimeError, FileNotFoundError) as exc:
        raise SystemExit(
            f"--all-open needs the gh CLI, authenticated, to list open PRs. "
            f"Name the candidates by ref instead, or set GH_TOKEN.\n{exc}")
    out = []
    for pr in json.loads(p.stdout):
        if pr.get("isDraft"):
            continue
        out.append(Candidate(f"#{pr['number']}", pr["headRefOid"],
                             sha=pr["headRefOid"], pr=pr["number"],
                             title=pr["title"]))
    return out


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# scratch clone


class Scratch:
    """A private clone. `--shared` means the objects are borrowed, not copied,
    so this costs a fraction of a second even on a repository this size."""

    def __init__(self, repo: pathlib.Path, work_dir=None, keep=False):
        self.repo, self.keep = repo, keep
        self.tmp = pathlib.Path(work_dir) if work_dir else pathlib.Path(
            tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="merge-check-"))
        self.dir = self.tmp / "tree"

    def __enter__(self):
        if not (self.dir / ".git").exists():
            self.dir.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            sh(["git", "clone", "--shared", "--no-checkout", "--quiet",
                str(self.repo), str(self.dir)])
        sh(["git", "config", "user.email", "merge-check@localhost"], cwd=self.dir)
        sh(["git", "config", "user.name", "merge-check"], cwd=self.dir)
        return self

    def __exit__(self, *exc):
        if not self.keep:
            shutil.rmtree(self.tmp, ignore_errors=True)

    def have(self, sha):
        return sh(["git", "cat-file", "-e", f"{sha}^{{commit}}"],
                  cwd=self.dir, check=False).returncode == 0

    def try_fetch(self, source, refspec, local):
        """Best-effort fetch of one ref. Returns the sha or None."""
        p = sh(["git", "fetch", "--quiet", "--no-tags", source,
                f"+{refspec}:{local}"], cwd=self.dir, check=False)
        return self.resolve(local) if p.returncode == 0 else None

    def find(self, ref, sources, local):
        """Resolve `ref` in the clone, else fetch it from `sources` in order.

        `git clone` maps the source's refs/heads/* and nothing else, so a base
        branch the caller only holds as a remote-tracking ref, or a PR head it
        holds under refs/remotes/pull/*, is reachable by sha but has no name
        here. Both spellings are tried before the network is."""
        for spelling in (ref, f"origin/{ref}"):
            sha = self.resolve(spelling)
            if sha:
                return sha
        for source, refspec in sources:
            if not source:
                continue
            sha = self.try_fetch(source, refspec, local)
            if sha:
                return sha
        return None

    def resolve(self, ref):
        p = sh(["git", "rev-parse", "--verify", f"{ref}^{{commit}}"],
               cwd=self.dir, check=False)
        return p.stdout.strip() if p.returncode == 0 else None

    def checkout(self, sha):
        sh(["git", "checkout", "--quiet", "--detach", "--force", sha],
           cwd=self.dir)
        sh(["git", "clean", "-qfdx"], cwd=self.dir)

    def merge(self, base_sha, shas):
        """Merge `shas` onto `base_sha` in order. Returns (commit, conflict).

        `conflict` is the index of the candidate that would not merge textually
        — git already reports those, but the caller needs to exclude it from
        the semantic search rather than run the suite on a conflicted tree."""
        self.checkout(base_sha)
        for i, sha in enumerate(shas):
            p = sh(["git", "merge", "--no-edit", "--no-ff", "-m",
                    "merge-check", sha], cwd=self.dir, check=False)
            if p.returncode != 0:
                sh(["git", "merge", "--abort"], cwd=self.dir, check=False)
                return None, i
        return self.resolve("HEAD"), None


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# checks


def run_check(scr: Scratch, name: str, j: int):
    """(verdict, detail lines) for one check on whatever is checked out."""
    if name in LINT_CHECKS:
        cmd, needs = LINT_CHECKS[name]
        if not (scr.dir / needs).exists():
            return ABSENT, []
        p = sh(cmd, cwd=scr.dir, check=False)
        detail = [ln for ln in plain(p.stdout + p.stderr).splitlines()
                  if ln.strip()][-8:]
        return (GREEN if p.returncode == 0 else RED), detail

    p = sh(["python3", "tests/parallel", "-j", str(j), name],
           cwd=scr.dir, check=False)
    text = plain(p.stdout + p.stderr)
    if p.returncode == 2 and "no test module matches" in text:
        return ABSENT, []
    if p.returncode == 0:
        return GREEN, []
    detail = [ln for ln in text.splitlines()
              if ln.startswith(("FAIL: ", "ERROR: "))]
    return RED, detail or text.splitlines()[-6:]


def sweep(scr: Scratch, j: int, only=None):
    """Red check names on the currently checked-out tree.

    Returns (red, unattributable). `unattributable` is set when the runner
    failed without naming a module — the caller must not silently call that
    green."""
    if only:
        red = []
        for name in only:
            verdict, _ = run_check(scr, name, j)
            if verdict == RED:
                red.append(name)
        return red, False

    p = sh(["python3", "tests/parallel", "-j", str(j)], cwd=scr.dir, check=False)
    text = plain(p.stdout + p.stderr)
    red = [m.group(1) for line in text.splitlines()
           if (m := RED_MODULE.match(line.strip()))]
    unattributable = p.returncode != 0 and not red
    for name in LINT_CHECKS:
        verdict, _ = run_check(scr, name, j)
        if verdict == RED:
            red.append(name)
    return red, unattributable


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def main() -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        description=__doc__.splitlines()[0],
        formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter,
        epilog="\n".join(__doc__.splitlines()[1:]))
    ap.add_argument("candidates", nargs="*", metavar="NAME=REF",
                    help="a change to include, e.g. a=origin/coding/task-100")
    ap.add_argument("--base", default=None,
                    help="branch or ref every candidate is merged onto "
                         "(default: the current branch)")
    ap.add_argument("--pr", action="append", type=int, default=[],
                    help="include this PR by number (repeatable)")
    ap.add_argument("--all-open", action="store_true",
                    help="include every open, non-draft PR targeting --base")
    ap.add_argument("--checks", nargs="*", default=None,
                    help="restrict to these checks instead of the whole suite "
                         "— for reproducing a known pair, not for gating")
    ap.add_argument("-j", type=int, default=4, help="workers per suite run")
    ap.add_argument("--work-dir", default=None,
                    help="reuse this scratch dir instead of a temp one")
    ap.add_argument("--keep", action="store_true", help="keep the scratch dir")
    args = ap.parse_args()
    sys.stdout.reconfigure(line_buffering=True)

    repo = pathlib.Path(sh(["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"]).stdout.strip())
    base_ref = args.base or sh(
        ["git", "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"], cwd=repo).stdout.strip()

    cands = []
    for spec in args.candidates:
        if "=" not in spec:
            print(f"candidate must be NAME=REF, got {spec!r}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        name, _, ref = spec.partition("=")
        cands.append(Candidate(name, ref))
    for n in args.pr:
        cands.append(Candidate(f"#{n}", f"refs/pull/{n}/head", pr=n))
    if args.all_open:
        cands.extend(open_prs(repo, base_ref))

    if len(cands) < 1:
        print("nothing to check: give candidates, --pr, or --all-open")
        return 0

    url = sh(["git", "remote", "get-url", "origin"], cwd=repo,
             check=False).stdout.strip()

    with Scratch(repo, args.work_dir, args.keep) as scr:
        base_sha = scr.find(base_ref, [
            (str(repo), f"refs/remotes/origin/{base_ref}"),
            (str(repo), f"refs/heads/{base_ref}"),
            (url, f"refs/heads/{base_ref}"),
        ], "refs/merge-check/base")
        if not base_sha:
            print(f"cannot resolve base {base_ref!r}", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2

        # The scratch clone borrows the caller's object database, so a PR head
        # the caller has already fetched is reachable by sha even though no ref
        # in the clone points at it. That is the path CI takes: the workflow
        # fetches refs/pull/*/head once, with the credentials it already has,
        # and this never needs the network.
        for c in cands:
            sha = c.sha if c.sha and scr.have(c.sha) else None
            if not sha:
                sources = []
                if c.pr:
                    sources = [(str(repo), f"refs/remotes/pull/{c.pr}/head"),
                               (str(repo), f"refs/remotes/pull/{c.pr}"),
                               (str(repo), f"refs/pull/{c.pr}/head"),
                               (url, f"refs/pull/{c.pr}/head")]
                sha = scr.find(c.ref, sources,
                               f"refs/merge-check/cand/{len(cands)}-{c.name}")
            if not sha:
                print(f"cannot resolve candidate {c.name} ({c.ref}) — if it is "
                      f"a PR, fetch refs/pull/*/head first", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
            c.sha = sha

        print(f"merge-check · base {base_ref} @ {base_sha[:8]}")
        for c in cands:
            print(f"  {c.name:<8} {c.sha[:8]}  {c.title}")
        print()

        note(f"merging {len(cands)} candidate(s) onto {base_sha[:8]}")
        combined, conflict_at = scr.merge(base_sha, [c.sha for c in cands])
        if conflict_at is not None:
            blocker = cands[conflict_at]
            others = ", ".join(c.name for c in cands[:conflict_at]) or base_ref
            print(f"TEXTUAL CONFLICT — {blocker.name} does not merge with "
                  f"{others}. git reports this one; nothing semantic was run.")
            return 1

        note("running the suite on the merged result")
        red, unattributable = sweep(scr, args.j, args.checks)
        if unattributable:
            print("the runner failed on the combined tree without naming a "
                  "module — cannot attribute; read the run above.")
            return 1
        if not red:
            n = len(cands)
            print(f"✓ the merged result of {n} change(s) is green — "
                  f"no pair disagrees.")
            return 0

        # Subtract what was already broken. Without this every pair is guilty.
        note(f"{len(red)} check(s) red — baselining them on the base alone")
        pre_existing, live = [], []
        scr.checkout(base_sha)
        for name in red:
            verdict, _ = run_check(scr, name, args.j)
            (pre_existing if verdict == RED else live).append(name)

        if pre_existing:
            print("pre-existing on the base — attributed to nobody:")
            for name in pre_existing:
                print(f"  · {name}")
            print()
        if not live:
            print("✓ nothing new is red on the merged result — every "
                  "failure above was already red on the base.")
            return 0

        # Solo, then pairs. One merge per tree, every live check on it — the
        # merge is cheap but the checks are not, and re-merging per check
        # would multiply both.
        note(f"{len(live)} newly red — running each candidate alone")
        solo = {name: {} for name in live}
        for c in cands:
            scr.merge(base_sha, [c.sha])
            for name in live:
                solo[name][c.name] = run_check(scr, name, args.j)

        # A check already red with one candidate alone is that candidate's own
        # regression. It is named on its own and takes no part in pair search,
        # so an unrelated open PR is never dragged in beside it.
        pairs = {}
        pairwise = [n for n in live
                    if not any(solo[n][c.name][0] == RED for c in cands)]
        if pairwise:
            note(f"searching pairs for {len(pairwise)} check(s)")
            for a, b in itertools.combinations(cands, 2):
                _, conflict_at = scr.merge(base_sha, [a.sha, b.sha])
                if conflict_at is not None:
                    continue
                for name in pairwise:
                    pairs[(a.name, b.name, name)] = run_check(
                        scr, name, args.j)

        failed = False
        for name in live:
            broken_alone = [c for c in cands if solo[name][c.name][0] == RED]
            if broken_alone:
                failed = True
                for c in broken_alone:
                    print(f"BROKEN ON ITS OWN — {c.name} · {name}")
                    for ln in solo[name][c.name][1]:
                        print(f"    {ln}")
                    print()
                continue

            found = False
            for a, b in itertools.combinations(cands, 2):
                verdict, detail = pairs.get((a.name, b.name, name), (GREEN, []))
                if verdict != RED:
                    continue
                found = failed = True
                print(f"CONFLICTING PAIR — {a.name} × {b.name} · {name}")
                print(f"    {a.name} alone: {solo[name][a.name][0]}   "
                      f"{b.name} alone: {solo[name][b.name][0]}   "
                      f"together: red")
                if a.title:
                    print(f"    {a.name}  {a.title}")
                if b.title:
                    print(f"    {b.name}  {b.title}")
                for ln in detail:
                    print(f"    {ln}")
                print()

            if not found:
                failed = True
                names = ", ".join(c.name for c in cands)
                if len(cands) > 2:
                    print(f"CANNOT ATTRIBUTE TO A PAIR — {name} is red with "
                          f"all of [{names}] merged, green with any one of "
                          f"them, and green with every pair. It takes three "
                          f"or more together; this will not guess which.\n")
                else:
                    print(f"CANNOT ATTRIBUTE — {name} is red with [{names}] "
                          f"merged and green every other way, including the "
                          f"one pair that IS that tree. That is not possible "
                          f"deterministically: re-run it, and suspect the "
                          f"check itself before suspecting either change.\n")

        return 1 if failed else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
